{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-81779",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the line $y=\\frac{x}{3}$ with the line $y=3.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$6\\le x\\le 9.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261478/assets/cd1948c65b6bec22f43b07fce2bcc54840f026457dda49115b4a0f8adf37a381.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,6\\le x\\le 9,\\,\\frac{x}{3}\\le y\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dA={\\int }_{6}^{9}{\\int }_{x/3}^{3}dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{9}{\\int }_{x/3}^{3}dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{6}^{9}[{\\int }_{x/3}^{3}dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{9}[y]_{x/3}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{9}(3-\\frac{x}{3})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{9}(3-\\frac{x}{3})\\,dx & ={[3x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}]}_{6}^{9} \\\\  & =27-\\frac{81}{6}-(18-\\frac{36}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
